---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="PTCL Packages & Speeds — Which Speed Do You Need?"
  description="PTCL broadband and Flash Fiber speed tiers explained: what 6 Mbps, 10 Mbps, 20 Mbps, 50 Mbps and 100 Mbps are actually good for, and how to pick the right package."
  canonical="/packages"
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / Packages</p>
      <h1>PTCL Packages &amp; Speeds</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        PTCL sells broadband by download speed — but the number on the package is a
        maximum, and the right speed depends on your household. Here's how the tiers
        compare.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Prices change often.</strong> This page compares speeds, not prices —
          PTCL revises package prices and promotions regularly. Check the current price of
          any package at <a href="https://www.ptcl.com.pk" rel="noopener" target="_blank">ptcl.com.pk</a>
          or by calling PTCL helpline <strong>1218</strong> before you decide.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>PTCL broadband speed tiers</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="data">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Speed</th><th>Best for</th><th>Household size</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>4 – 6 Mbps</strong></td><td>Browsing, WhatsApp, YouTube in SD/HD, email</td><td>1–2 people, light use</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>8 – 10 Mbps</strong></td><td>HD streaming, video calls, online classes</td><td>2–3 people</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>15 – 20 Mbps</strong></td><td>Full HD on 2–3 screens, work from home</td><td>3–4 people</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>30 – 50 Mbps</strong></td><td>4K streaming, gaming, large downloads</td><td>4–6 people</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>100 Mbps+</strong></td><td>Everything at once — 4K, gaming, calls, downloads</td><td>Large homes &amp; small offices</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>DSL vs Flash Fiber — what's the difference?</h2>
      <p>
        PTCL offers two kinds of home connection, and they behave very differently:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>DSL broadband</strong> runs over your telephone line. Speeds are lower
          (typically up to 20&nbsp;Mbps) and depend heavily on your distance from the
          exchange. Upload speeds are much slower than download speeds.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Flash Fiber (FTTH)</strong> runs over fibre optic cable to your home.
          Speeds go far higher (30&nbsp;Mbps to 250&nbsp;Mbps), upload is much faster, and
          distance from the exchange barely matters. If fibre is available on your street,
          it is almost always the better connection.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Not sure which one you have? If your connection comes through a phone line and a
        splitter, it's DSL. If a thin fibre cable runs to a small white ONT box, it's fibre.
      </p>

      <h2>How to choose the right speed</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Count your simultaneous users.</strong> Two people streaming in HD need
          roughly 10–15&nbsp;Mbps between them; add 25&nbsp;Mbps for each 4K stream.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Think about uploads.</strong> If you work from home, teach online or
          upload videos, upload speed matters as much as download — fibre wins here.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Test what you have first.</strong>
          <a href="/speed-test">Run a speed test</a> on your current package. If you're
          already getting close to your package speed and still struggling, you need a
          faster package. If you're getting far less than you pay for, fix the connection
          before upgrading — see
          <a href="/guides/improve-ptcl-speed">how to improve your PTCL speed</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Don't overbuy.</strong> A 100&nbsp;Mbps line is wasted if two people only
          browse and watch YouTube. The jump from 10 to 20&nbsp;Mbps is felt far more than
          the jump from 50 to 100.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Before you upgrade</h2>
      <p>
        Ask PTCL these three things: is Flash Fiber available at your address, what upload
        speed comes with the package, and is there an installation or device charge. Then
        test your new connection the day it's installed — if it doesn't match the package,
        report it while the installation is fresh.
      </p>

      <div class="cta-band">
        <h2>Check your current speed first</h2>
        <p>Thirty seconds now can save you from paying for speed you don't need — or don't get.</p>
        <a class="btn" href="/speed-test">Run the Speed Test</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
